<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-dist-pt-hyperplane">
<title>Distance from a point to a hyperplane in <m> \R^n </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	use an analogous method to the one for <xref ref="exercisegroup-orthog-dist-pt-plane-by-eqn" text="type-local">Exercises</xref>
	to determine the distance in <m> \R^n </m> from the point <m> R </m> to the <term>hyperplane</term> <m> H </m>.
	(See the preamble to <xref ref="exercisegroup-orthog-normal-hyperplane-eqn-Rn" text="type-local">Exercises</xref>.)
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement>
		<p><m> R(1,0,-6,6) </m></p>
		<p><m> H \colon \:\: 5 x_1 + 2 x_2 - 5 x_3 + 2 x_4 = 0 </m></p>
	</statement>

	<solution>

		<p>
		Since the equation for <m> H </m> is homogeneous,
		it passes through the origin,
		and we can create a vector from the hyperplane to <m> R </m> by using the origin as the initial point:
		<md> \uvec{u} = \abray{OR} = (1,0,-6,6) </md>.
		Also, from the hyperplane equation we obtain a normal vector for <m> H </m>: <md> \uvec{n} = (5,2,-5,2) </md>.
		The distance from <m> R </m> to the hyperplane will be the norm of the orthogonal projection of <m> \uvec{u} </m> onto <m> \uvec{n} </m>.
		</p>

		<p>
		So first compute that projection:
		<md> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{n} = \left( \frac{235}{58}, \frac{47}{29}, - \frac{235}{58}, \frac{47}{29} \right) </md>.
		Finally, compute the distance from <m> R </m> to the hyperplane as the norm of <m> \uvec{p} </m>:
		<md> d = \unorm{p} = \frac{47}{\sqrt{58}} </md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>
		<p><m> R(1,3,-3,2) </m></p>
		<p><m> H \colon \:\: 6 x_1 - 2 x_2 + 2 x_3 - 4 x_4 = 3 </m></p>
	</statement>

	<solution>

		<p>
		By inspection, the point <m> P(1/2,0,0,0) </m> is on <m> H </m> because its coordinates satisfy the hyperplane equation.
		Then we can create a vector from the hyperplane to <m> R </m> by using <m> P </m> as the initial point:
		<md> \uvec{u} = \abray{PR} = \left( \frac{1}{2}, 3, -3, 2 \right) </md>.
		Also, from the hyperplane equation we obtain a normal vector for <m> \Pi </m>: <md> \uvec{n} = (6,-2,2,-4) </md>.
		The distance from <m> R </m> to the hyperplane will be the norm of the orthogonal projection of <m> \uvec{u} </m> onto <m> \uvec{n} </m>.
		</p>

		<p>
		So first compute that projection:
		<md> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{n} = \left( - \frac{17}{10}, \frac{17}{30}, - \frac{17}{30}, \frac{17}{15} \right) </md>.
		Finally, compute the distance from <m> R </m> to the hyperplane as the norm of <m> \uvec{p} </m>:
		<md> d = \unorm{p} = \frac{17}{2 \sqrt{15}} </md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>
		<p><m> R(2,-3,6,5,1) </m></p>
		<p><m> H \colon \:\: x_1 - 2 x_2 + 3 x_4 + 5 x_5 = 0 </m></p>
	</statement>

	<solution>

		<p>
		Since the equation for <m> H </m> is homogeneous,
		it passes through the origin,
		and we can create a vector from the hyperplane to <m> R </m> by using the origin as the initial point:
		<md> \uvec{u} = \abray{OR} = (2,-3,6,5,1) </md>.
		Also, from the hyperplane equation we obtain a normal vector for <m> H </m>: <md> \uvec{n} = (1,-2,0,3,5) </md>.
		The distance from <m> R </m> to the hyperplane will be the norm of the orthogonal projection of <m> \uvec{u} </m> onto <m> \uvec{n} </m>.
		</p>

		<p>
		So first compute that projection:
		<md> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{n} = \left( \frac{28}{39}, - \frac{56}{39}, 0, \frac{28}{13}, \frac{140}{39}\right) </md>.
		Finally, compute the distance from <m> R </m> to the hyperplane as the norm of <m> \uvec{p} </m>:
		<md> d = \unorm{p} = \frac{28}{\sqrt{39}} </md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>
		<p><m> R(-6,1,1,0,-5) </m></p>
		<p><m> H \colon \:\: 6 x_1 + 3 x_2 + 3 x_3 - 2 x_4 - 3 x_5 = 1 </m></p>
	</statement>

	<solution>

		<p>
		By inspection, the point <m> P(0,0,1,1,0) </m> is on <m> H </m> because its coordinates satisfy the hyperplane equation.
		Then we can create a vector from the hyperplane to <m> R </m> by using <m> P </m> as the initial point:
		<md> \uvec{u} = \abray{PR} = (-6, 1, 0, -1, -5) </md>.
		Also, from the hyperplane equation we obtain a normal vector for <m> \Pi </m>: <md> \uvec{n} = (6,3,3,-2,-3) </md>.
		The distance from <m> R </m> to the hyperplane will be the norm of the orthogonal projection of <m> \uvec{u} </m> onto <m> \uvec{n} </m>.
		</p>

		<p>
		So first compute that projection:
		<md> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{n} = \left( - \frac{96}{67}, - \frac{48}{67}, - \frac{48}{67}, \frac{32}{67}, \frac{48}{67} \right) </md>.
		Finally, compute the distance from <m> R </m> to the hyperplane as the norm of <m> \uvec{p} </m>:
		<md> d = \unorm{p} = \frac{16}{\sqrt{67}} </md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
